1
00:00:00,099 --> 00:00:02,407
Think about what passes through email every day:

2
00:00:02,442 --> 00:00:06,120
password resets, business documents, and private conversations.

3
00:00:06,846 --> 00:00:09,463
Now imagine an employee logging into their mailbox,

4
00:00:09,547 --> 00:00:12,139
but the connection sends their login details before

5
00:00:12,014 --> 00:00:15,877
encryption begins. Someone able to observe that traffic

6
00:00:15,751 --> 00:00:17,492
could capture those credentials.

7
00:00:18,218 --> 00:00:20,638
The email service might still work normally.

8
00:00:21,363 --> 00:00:23,684
To the employee, nothing would look wrong.

9
00:00:24,694 --> 00:00:27,546
And exposed logins are only one possibility.

10
00:00:28,259 --> 00:00:31,147
A connection might use outdated encryption, present an

11
00:00:31,022 --> 00:00:33,972
expired security certificate, or fail to switch to

12
00:00:33,834 --> 00:00:35,168
encryption when it should.

13
00:00:35,894 --> 00:00:39,152
These weaknesses can put accounts and sensitive communication

14
00:00:39,027 --> 00:00:42,273
at risk. That’s why “the email was delivered”

15
00:00:42,185 --> 00:00:45,196
isn’t enough. We also need to know whether

16
00:00:45,071 --> 00:00:47,676
the connection carrying it was properly protected.

17
00:00:48,674 --> 00:00:50,909
But answering that isn’t straightforward.

18
00:00:51,634 --> 00:00:54,103
The evidence can be scattered across thousands of

19
00:00:53,978 --> 00:00:58,124
network packets. Someone has to reconstruct the conversation

20
00:00:57,999 --> 00:01:00,530
and identify where protection began or where it

21
00:01:00,404 --> 00:01:03,601
failed. The challenge is turning that traffic into

22
00:01:03,463 --> 00:01:06,019
clear answers: what happened, why does it matter,

23
00:01:06,029 --> 00:01:07,264
and what should we fix?

24
00:01:08,249 --> 00:01:10,952
That’s what we’re building SecureMailScope to help with.

25
00:01:11,678 --> 00:01:13,703
Think of it as a safety inspection for

26
00:01:13,578 --> 00:01:17,231
email connections. You provide a recording of network

27
00:01:17,093 --> 00:01:20,105
traffic, and it reconstructs the connections, checks for

28
00:01:19,979 --> 00:01:23,040
observable weaknesses, and presents explained findings.

29
00:01:23,754 --> 00:01:26,864
You get supporting evidence, suggested fixes, and an

30
00:01:26,739 --> 00:01:31,292
exportable report. Analysis runs locally, and reports leave

31
00:01:31,167 --> 00:01:33,019
out credentials and message bodies.

32
00:01:33,732 --> 00:01:35,967
The goal is to make hidden connection risks

33
00:01:35,841 --> 00:01:38,310
visible, understandable, and actionable.

34
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:41,174
Now, let’s look under the hood.

35
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:45,195
This cleartext authentication sample is a generated laboratory

36
00:01:45,070 --> 00:01:47,588
capture, processed through the same pipeline as an

37
00:01:47,463 --> 00:01:49,821
uploaded PCAP or PCAPNG file.

38
00:01:50,547 --> 00:01:53,891
The backend extracts packets using TShark, with Scapy

39
00:01:53,766 --> 00:01:57,764
as a fallback. It reconstructs TCP streams, tracks

40
00:01:57,639 --> 00:02:01,613
retransmissions and gaps, and parses SMTP, IMAP, and

41
00:02:01,475 --> 00:02:05,449
POP3 conversations. That sequence lets us check whether

42
00:02:05,323 --> 00:02:08,014
authentication happened before or after encryption.

43
00:02:09,021 --> 00:02:12,711
In this session, authentication occurs before encryption, triggering

44
00:02:12,586 --> 00:02:14,734
the critical AUTH-001 rule.

45
00:02:15,447 --> 00:02:17,645
We can trace the finding to its originating

46
00:02:17,507 --> 00:02:20,432
frame, inspect the redacted command, and review the

47
00:02:20,295 --> 00:02:24,836
recommended fix. The STARTTLS failure scenario demonstrates a

48
00:02:24,698 --> 00:02:27,981
related problem: the client requests encryption, the upgrade

49
00:02:27,856 --> 00:02:30,756
fails, and authentication continues in plaintext.

50
00:02:31,470 --> 00:02:34,346
Requesting protection doesn’t mean it was established.

51
00:02:35,071 --> 00:02:37,195
We check the observed conversation.

52
00:02:38,187 --> 00:02:42,358
SecureMailScope also checks observable TLS versions, cipher suites,

53
00:02:42,369 --> 00:02:45,997
and forward secrecy. When a certificate is visible,

54
00:02:46,044 --> 00:02:49,056
it checks expiry against the capture timestamp, hostname

55
00:02:48,931 --> 00:02:51,264
matching, key strength, and chain trust.

56
00:02:51,990 --> 00:02:53,706
There are visibility limits.

57
00:02:54,407 --> 00:02:58,517
TLS 1.3 encrypts certificate contents, so those details

58
00:02:58,391 --> 00:03:02,784
may remain unknown. Incomplete sessions remain unassessed rather

59
00:03:02,647 --> 00:03:04,819
than receiving a misleading clean score.

60
00:03:05,838 --> 00:03:09,479
Alongside explicit rules, we provide a separate machine-learning

61
00:03:09,341 --> 00:03:13,179
signal. A Random Forest classifies risk, while an

62
00:03:13,054 --> 00:03:17,114
Isolation Forest flags unusual combinations of normalized cryptographic

63
00:03:16,988 --> 00:03:20,752
features. The models analyze these features, not raw

64
00:03:20,627 --> 00:03:24,527
email content. They use synthetic training data, so

65
00:03:24,401 --> 00:03:28,017
they demonstrate the approach without establishing real-world accuracy.

66
00:03:28,743 --> 00:03:31,298
The score displays rule deductions and a separate

67
00:03:31,173 --> 00:03:34,012
machine-learning penalty, allowing us to inspect how the

68
00:03:33,874 --> 00:03:35,319
result was calculated.

69
00:03:36,331 --> 00:03:39,194
Finally, we can export the assessment as JSON,

70
00:03:39,254 --> 00:03:43,339
HTML, or PDF, including evidence references and recommended

71
00:03:43,213 --> 00:03:48,198
fixes. SecureMailScope connects packet-level observations to conclusions

72
00:03:48,073 --> 00:03:50,135
people can inspect and act on helping us

73
00:03:50,010 --> 00:03:52,737
look beyond whether email works, and understand how

74
00:03:52,612 --> 00:03:53,909
securely it connects.
